Short:        AI Assistant Shell for Commodore Amiga (Ollama & Cloud Bridge)
Author:       Patu^Xenium^AberrationCreations
Uploader:     patuwwy@gmail.com
Type:         comm/misc
Version:      ${VERSION}
Architecture: m68k-amigaos
Requires:     AmigaOS 2.04+ (Kickstart 37+), bsdsocket.library, 2MB+ Fast RAM

AMAI (Amiga AI) - Amiga AI Shell
================================
AMAI is an interactive AI coding and assistant Shell client for Commodore
Amiga (in the style of Claude Code / Gemini CLI / Aider). It enables pair
programming directly on AmigaOS (C, 680x0 ASM, ARexx, Amiga E, AmigaDOS),
reading/writing files, patching code, and executing AmigaDOS commands with
safety approval modes.

It connects through a lightweight bridge server on your modern PC/Mac/Linux
machine, allowing you to use local models (Ollama, LM Studio) or cloud AI
providers (Google Gemini, OpenAI, OpenRouter) with full tool-calling support.

Included Executables:
---------------------
This archive contains standalone self-contained executables created with
the NodeAmiga compiler (-compile). They do NOT require NodeAmiga or external
JS files to run:
- amai       : Universal build for MC68000 / MC68010 (all Amiga models)
- amai_020   : Optimized for MC68020 / MC68030 (A1200 / A3000 / 030 boards)
- amai_040   : Optimized for MC68040 (A4000 / 040 accelerators)
- amai_060   : Optimized for MC68060 (060 accelerators - maximum speed)

Quick Start Guide:
==================

1. On your PC / Mac / Linux (Host Bridge):
------------------------------------------
AMAI communicates with AI models via a lightweight Node.js bridge server:
a) Local models (Ollama):
   - Install Ollama (https://ollama.com) and pull your model:
     ollama run qwen2.5:latest (or qwen3.8:latest)
b) Cloud models (Google Gemini / OpenAI / OpenRouter):
   - Copy pc/.env.example to pc/.env and set your API keys:
     GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key
     OPENAI_API_KEY=your_openai_api_key
     OPENROUTER_API_KEY=your_openrouter_api_key
   - You can also configure DEFAULT_MODEL in pc/.env.
c) Run the bridge server in the amai project directory on your PC:
   node pc/server.js
   (or double-click start_bridge.bat on Windows, or ./start_bridge.sh on Linux/macOS)
   The bridge will automatically log active providers and listen on port 11435.

2. On your Amiga:
-----------------
a) Ensure a TCP/IP stack is running (Roadshow, Miami, AmiTCP, ImpBox, or
   WinUAE bsdsocket emulation) providing bsdsocket.library.
b) Extract this archive to your preferred location (e.g. DH0:Tools/amai/).
c) Edit amai.config.json to set your PC's IP address:
   "host": "192.168.1.50"
   (or in WinUAE use "127.0.0.1")
d) Run AMAI from the AmigaDOS Shell:
   amai
   Or specify host and model on the command line:
   amai -h 192.168.1.50 -m gemini-flash-latest

During the interactive session, you can use built-in slash commands such
as /help, /status, /host, /model, /mode, /encoding, /save, and /clear.
Use "/model" to list all available local and cloud models.

About NodeAmiga Runtime:
------------------------
AMAI is developed and compiled using NodeAmiga, a JavaScript runtime
environment and compiler for Classic AmigaOS:
- Author: Juen / R3D + Appendix + Nah-Kolor
- Documentation: http://juen.in/NodeAmiga/
- Aminet package: https://aminet.net/package/dev/lang/NodeAmiga
- Support: http://juen.in/support/
